#open-question(title: [Can Mediator-Arm Response Profiles Define a Reproducible "Four-Arm" MCAS Subtype Map?])[
The four pharmacologically distinct mediator arms — H1/PAF (blocked by rupatadine, a dual H1 + platelet-activating-factor antagonist @Rehman2026RupatadineMeta), H2 (famotidine), CysLT1 (leukotriene-receptor antagonists), and cholinergic (acetylcholinesterase inhibition or antimuscarinic agents) — could in principle be probed independently, and a patient's pattern of responses across them might cluster into reproducible MCAS subtypes (a "four-arm mediator map"). A substantial fraction of POTS patients — 36.3% in a 19-study meta-analysis, though MCAS diagnostic criteria vary — report comorbid MCAS @Kulin2026POTSGIMeta, which would give a large candidate probe population in whom such clustering would be informative. But response-profile clustering across mediator arms is not an established method: no study has systematically measured differential response to all four arms within the same MCAS/POTS patients, so it is unknown whether the clusters would be stable, reproducible, or clinically actionable.

This is an open question; no certainty is assigned. The POTS–MCAS overlap is quantified and the arm pharmacology is real, but the clustering method itself is unvalidated.

*Falsifiable prediction:* In a within-subject N-of-1 or crossover design across the four arms, patients' response profiles cluster into a small, stable number of subtypes (for example H1/PAF-dominant or CysLT1-dominant) that reproduce across independent cohorts and predict the direction of future response; if profiles are continuous with no reproducible clustering, a categorical "four-arm map" is not supported.

*Consequence:* A validated mediator-arm map would convert MCAS treatment from trial-and-error to a testable stratification, directing each patient to the arm their own response profile implicates — but until clustering is shown to reproduce, the map remains a research scaffold, not a clinical algorithm.
] <oq:four-arm-mast-cell-mediator-map>
